@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Cloud cluster with load balancer, app tier, cache, and DB.
'   usecase:      Typical cloud web app — LB fronting replicated app nodes,
'                 a shared cache, and a persistent database.
'   complexity:   medium
'   domain:       deployment
' ============================================================================
title Example Deployment Diagram - Medium Cloud Cluster

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' This diagram grows the simple case into a realistic cloud topology:
'
'   Internet -> Load Balancer -> [App Node 1, App Node 2, App Node 3]
'                                  (each talks to Cache and DB)
'
' New ideas demonstrated here:
'   * `cloud` shape      — abstract "the internet" / a cloud provider region
'   * MULTIPLE nodes of the same kind (an app TIER, not one box)
'   * Protocol labels on every arrow (HTTPS, TCP/Redis, JDBC)
'   * A cache node that all app nodes share
'   * Artifacts deployed onto each node (binary versioning visible)
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

' "The internet" — use `cloud` for abstract external networks.
' End users live out here.
cloud "Internet" as net {
  actor "End User" as user
}

' Load balancer — a <<device>> that terminates TLS and forwards.
' Note the OS artifact modeled as <<executionEnvironment>>: the LB runs
' ON an OS, and the LB software is itself deployed onto that OS.
node "Load Balancer" as lb <<device>> {
  artifact "Ubuntu 22.04" as lbos <<executionEnvironment>>
  artifact "HAProxy 2.8" as haproxy <<deploy>>
}

' App tier — THREE nodes behind the LB. Showing more than one is the whole
' point of a "cluster": horizontal scale, redundancy, rolling deploys.
node "App Node 1" as app1 <<device>> {
  artifact "UML-IDE Backend\n1.0.0.jar" as b1 <<deploy>>
}
node "App Node 2" as app2 <<device>> {
  artifact "UML-IDE Backend\n1.0.0.jar" as b2 <<deploy>>
}
node "App Node 3" as app3 <<device>> {
  artifact "UML-IDE Backend\n1.0.0.jar" as b3 <<deploy>>
}

' Cache — a single shared Redis instance. All app nodes read/write here.
' Using the `database` shape for a cache is common; you can also use `node`.
database "Redis Cache" as cache <<database>> {
  artifact "redis-server\n7.2" as redis <<deploy>>
}

' Database — the persistent store. One node here; real systems add replicas.
database "PostgreSQL" as db <<database>> {
  artifact "UML-IDE Schema\n1.0.0.sql" as schema <<deploy>>
}

' --- Communication paths -------------------------------------------------
' Label EVERY arrow with its protocol. This is what makes a deployment
' diagram useful for ops — you can read off the ports/protocols at a glance.

user --> lb : HTTPS
lb --> app1 : HTTP (round-robin)
lb --> app2 : HTTP (round-robin)
lb --> app3 : HTTP (round-robin)

' App nodes talk to the cache. Redis wire protocol runs over TCP:6379.
app1 --> cache : TCP/Redis :6379
app2 --> cache : TCP/Redis :6379
app3 --> cache : TCP/Redis :6379

' App nodes talk to the DB. Connection-pooled JDBC.
app1 --> db : JDBC :5432
app2 --> db : JDBC :5432
app3 --> db : JDBC :5432

' Teaching note: the cache is shared state.
note bottom of cache
  Shared cache: all three app nodes
  read/write the SAME keys.
  Cache invalidation must be
  cluster-aware.
end note

' Recap:
'   * SEVEN nodes: internet + LB + 3 app + cache + DB.
'   * Each app node hosts the SAME artifact version (a rolling deploy would
'     show mixed versions here).
'   * Every arrow has a protocol label — read it like a wiring diagram.

@enduml
